That is, if the rotation maps $ T_1 \to T_2 $, and $ T_2 \to T_1 $, or $ T_1 \to T_1 $

But can rotation by $ 3.6^\circ $ map $ T_1 \to T_2 $? Only if **$ T_1 \cong T_2 $** (since rotation preserves shape)

Similarly, **any** rotation of a triangle results in a triangle **congruent** to it.

So unless $ T_1 \cong T_2 $, we cannot have **rotation** mapping $ T_1 $ to $ T_2 $

Thus, **only if $ T_1 \cong T_2 $** can a rotation map $ T_1 \to T_2 $

Therefore, **under any rotation**, the **image** of any triangle is **a triangle congruent** to it

But since the only triangles are $ T_1 $ and $ T_2 $, and **rotation** produces **images**, unless **the image is already in $ \{T_1, T_2\} $** (i.e., the rotation maps $ T_1 \to T_2 $ or $ T_1 \to T_1 $), we get **more** triangles

We already ruled out **$ T_1 \to T_1 $** (by rotational symmetry, unless the triangle has high symmetry, which is not plausible)

Also, **can a rotation of $ T_1 $** by $ 3.6^\circ $ result in **$ T_2 $**?

Only **if $ T_1 \cong T_2 $** — because **rotation is an isometry**

Therefore, **the triangle $ T_1 $** and **image triangle** (under rotation) **must be congruent**

Hence, **$ T_2 $ must be congruent to $ T_1 $** — otherwise, the rotation produces a triangle **not congruent** to either of the original, or not in the set

Therefore, **the only way** for rotation to map triangle $ T_1 $ into **a triangle** in the dissection (which only has two) **without generating a new triangle** is if **$ T_1 $ and $ T_2 $** are **congruent**

Even more: Suppose $ T_1 $ is rotated by $ 3.6^\circ $ to a triangle in the dissection

That triangle **must be** congruent to $ T_1 $

Therefore, **any** such resulting triangle **is congruent** to $ T_1 $

Hence, **any triangle** that appears **under any rotation** must be **congruent** to $ T_1 $

Therefore, **the only possibility** that the **set** of triangles in the dissection has **only two** is that **they are congruent** to each other

Therefore, **contradiction** occurs if **$ T_1 \not\cong T_2 $**

Thus, **they must be congruent**

We can go further — reflection symmetry

Same argument holds. Any **reflection** across an axis of symmetry of the 100-gon (say, through a vertex) will reflect the whole dissection

Any triangle $ T $ will be **reflected** to **another piece**

Therefore, **reflection** of $ T_1 $ must yield **a triangle** in the dissection

Therefore, **$ \text{reflect}(T_1) $** is **a triangle** in the dissection

Therefore, **reflection** of $ T_1 $ must be **equal to** $ T_1 $ or $ T_2 $

If **reflection** sends $ T_1 \to T_2 $, then again, **$ T_1 \cong T_2 $** because reflection preserves shape

Thus, **in all cases**, regardless of the axis, unless $ T_1 \cong T_2 $, **either** we get **too many triangles** via rotation/reflection, **or** the images of the triangles are **not in the dissection**

Hence, the only **stable** configuration (with only two triangles) that satisfies symmetry of the regular 100-gon is when **the two triangles are congruent**

Therefore, **we conclude** that **any dissection** of a regular 100-gon into **parallelograms** and **two triangles** **must** have those **two triangles congruent**

Note: The **parallelograms** are **not** subject to such constraints as strongly, since they may be arranged asymmetrically. However, the **triangles** — being geometric figures in a symmetric structure — must be **symmetrically placed** or **paired** under symmetry, and unless the two are **congruent**, symmetry will generate **new, distinct triangles**.

Thus, **the triangle components must be congruent** to maintain **symmetry** and **consistency** of the dissection.

---

### Step 4: Special case — Could there be only two non-congruent triangles?

Only if **each rotation** maps triangle $ T_1 $ to itself or to another triangle **without generating new ones**

But:

- Rotation by $ 3.6^\circ $ implies that unless $ T_1 $ has rotational symmetry of order 100, its image is different.

- But **a triangle** in the plane with rotational symmetry of **order 100** (i.e., $ 3.6^\circ $) is **impossible** unless it's degenerate (e.g., line segment or point), because angles in a triangle sum to $ 180^\circ $

- And **rotational symmetry** by $ 3.6^\circ $ would require angles that repeat every $ 3.6^\circ $, which does not divide $ 180^\circ $ in a way that gives triangular symmetry

Therefore, **no triangle** can have rotational symmetry of $ 3.6^\circ $

Thus, **rotation** by $ 3.6^\circ $ sends **any triangle** to a **distinct triangle** (in position/orientation), unless it maps it to a **copy** of another triangle

Thus, in **any such dissection**, **unless** that image is **one** of the two allowed triangles, **we would get more than two triangles**

Hence, **rotation** implies that **every orbit** under rotation must be **contained** within the **two triangles**

Therefore, if **any** triangle is rotated, its image under rotation must be **one** of the two existing triangles

Therefore, the **set** of triangle images must be **closed** under rotation

So, unless the rotation sends triangle $ T_1 $ to **$ T_2 $**, and $ T_2 $ to $ T_1 $, or $ T_1 \to T_1 $, we get **too many**

But $ T_1 \to T_1 $ requires symmetry → impossible

Hence, the **only possibility** is that **$ T_1 \to T_2 $** and **$ T_2 \to T_1 $**

And thus **$ T_1 \cong T_2 $** (again, because isometry preserves congruence)

Therefore, **we are forced** to accept **$ T_1 \cong T_2 $**

Similarly, reflection symmetry leads to **same conclusion**

Thus, **no matter** how the dissection is made, **with only two triangles**, and within a **symmetric environment** like a **regular 100-gon**, **the triangles must be congruent**

Therefore, this completes the reasoning.

---

### Final Conclusion

Any dissection of a **regular 100-gon** into **parallelograms** and **two triangles** must preserve **rotational and reflectional symmetry** of the original polygon.

Because no non-degenerate triangle can be invariant under rotation by $ 3.6^\circ $, and such rotation would transform any triangle into a **distinct position**, unless it maps to **one** of the two existing triangles, we are forced to have **one triangle mapped to the other** under rotation or reflection.

Because **isometries** preserve congruence, **mapping one triangle to another implies congruence**.

Hence, the **only possible** configuration satisfying symmetry and having **only two triangles** is when **the two triangles are congruent**.

Thus, we have proven the required result.

